[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Linear Data Structures: Arrays, Strings, and Linked Lists in Problem Solving"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linear data structures form the backbone of efficient algorithm design and problem-solving. In this tutorial, we will explore arrays, strings, and linked lists, focusing on their memory layouts, time complexities, and common patterns used in coding interviews."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Arrays and Strings: Contiguous Memory Efficiency"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrays store elements in contiguous memory locations, allowing O(1) random access via indices. Strings are essentially arrays of characters, often requiring immutable operations in languages like Java and Python, or mutable buffers in C++."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Random Access: O(1) time complexity using the memory offset formula."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insertion and Deletion: O(N) time complexity due to the need to shift subsequent elements."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"def two_sum(nums, target):
    seen = {}
    for i, num in enumerate(nums):
        complement = target - num
        if complement in seen:
            return [seen[complement], i]
        seen[num] = i
    return []"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Two-Pointer Technique for Strings and Arrays"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The two-pointer pattern is heavily used in linear data structures. By maintaining two indices (e.g., left and right, or slow and fast), you can solve problems like palindrome validation or container with most water in O(N) time and O(1) space."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"bool isPalindrome(string s) {
    int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (!isalnum(s[left])) left++;
        else if (!isalnum(s[right])) right--;
        else if (tolower(s[left++]) != tolower(s[right--])) return false;
    }
    return true;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Linked Lists: Dynamic Memory and Pointers"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike arrays, linked lists consist of nodes where each node contains data and a reference (pointer) to the next node. They excel at insertions and deletions because no elements need to be shifted, but they lack O(1) random access."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Singly Linked List: Each node points only to the next node."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Doubly Linked List: Nodes contain pointers to both the next and previous nodes."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"class ListNode {
    constructor(val, next = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}

function reverseList(head) {
    let prev = null;
    let current = head;
    while (current !== null) {
        let nextTemp = current.next;
        current.next = prev;
        prev = current;
        current = nextTemp;
    }
    return prev;
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"When approaching a problem, first identify if the data can fit into an array or string for fast lookups and traversals. If your problem requires frequent insertions and deletions at arbitrary positions without shifting, choose a linked list. Always analyze the trade-offs between time complexity and space complexity."}]
